.TH LDD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ldd \- list the shared libraries an ELF file depends on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ldd
.I file
\&...
.br
.B ldd
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ldd
reports the shared-library dependencies of each 32-bit ELF
.IR file .
When more than one
.I file
is given, each report is preceded by a line holding the file name and a
colon.
.PP
The whole file is read and its program headers are examined.
.IP \(bu 2
A file with no
.B PT_DYNAMIC
segment is reported as
.RS
.IP
.EX
<tab>not a dynamic executable [\fIosabi\fP, e_type=\fIn\fP]
.EE
.RE
.IP
where
.I osabi
is
.BR SysV/native ,
.BR NetBSD ,
.BR Linux ,
.B FreeBSD
or
.B unknown
according to the
.B EI_OSABI
byte.
.IP \(bu
A file whose
.B PT_INTERP
is
.I /sbin/ld.so
(a native dynamic executable) is executed with
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS=1
in its environment and no arguments.
.BR ld.so (8)
then loads the complete dependency tree, prints each object with its
resolved path and real load address, and exits without running the
program.
See
.BR ld.so (8)
for the format.
If the child exits with status 127 (the file could not be executed, or
.B ld.so
hit a fatal error such as a missing library),
.B ldd
falls back to the method below.
.IP \(bu
Any other dynamic file (a shared library, or a Linux, FreeBSD or NetBSD
binary) is not executed.
.B ldd
prints a line
.RS
.IP
.EX
<tab>[interpreter: \fIpath\fP, OSABI=\fIosabi\fP]
.EE
.RE
.IP
(or just
.BI [OSABI= osabi ]
if there is no
.BR PT_INTERP ),
then, for each
.B DT_NEEDED
entry of the file itself,
.RS
.IP
.EX
<tab>\fIname\fP => \fIpath\fP (0x????????)
<tab>\fIname\fP => not found
.EE
.RE
.IP
Only direct dependencies are listed, and no load address is known.
Names are looked up as regular files in a fixed list of directories
chosen from the
.B EI_OSABI
byte and the interpreter name:
.RS
.TP 10
NetBSD
.IR /perso/netbsd/lib ,
.IR /perso/netbsd/usr/lib ,
.I /perso/netbsd/usr/pkg/lib
.TP
FreeBSD
.IR /perso/freebsd/lib ,
.I /perso/freebsd/usr/lib
.TP
Linux
.IR /perso/linux/lib ,
.I /perso/linux/usr/lib
.TP
otherwise
.IR /lib ,
.IR /usr/lib ,
.I /usr/local/lib
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message to standard error and exit with status 0.
Recognized only as the first argument; no other options exist, and any
other argument is taken as a file name.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I file
was examined, including files with missing dependencies.
.TP
.B 1
No
.I file
was given, or at least one
.I file
could not be read, was not a 32-bit ELF file, had malformed program
headers or had no usable
.BR DT_STRTAB .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ ldd /bin/ls
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR brandelf (1),
.BR readelf (1),
.BR ld.so (8)
.SH BUGS
For a native executable the dependency list comes from
.BR ld.so (8),
which writes it to standard error; it cannot be piped without
redirecting standard error.
.PP
A missing dependency does not change the exit status.
.PP
Native executables are run to obtain the listing.
.BR ld.so (8)
ignores
.B LD_TRACE_LOADED_OBJECTS
for set-user-ID and set-group-ID programs, so running
.B ldd
on such a program as an ordinary user starts the program itself.
.PP
Substrate's own brand,
.B ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE
(64), is reported as
.BR unknown .
.PP
The fallback method does not read
.I /etc/ld.so.conf
and does not follow indirect dependencies.
.PP
Only 32-bit ELF files are supported.
.PP
With several files and standard output not a terminal, the file-name
header line can appear after that file's listing, because output is
not flushed before the program is run.
